\section{What the static link leaves unfinished}

Mapping an image puts bytes at addresses; relocation makes particular fields inside those bytes hold useful values. The static linker can finish a branch between two known positions in one image: moving both positions by the same amount leaves their distance unchanged. But an initialized pointer to an object in the same image must contain that object's \emph{runtime} address. A pointer to a symbol supplied by a different image additionally needs the identity of the chosen definition. Levine's example in Chapter~8 adds a single displacement to the marked locations of an image loaded as a unit \cite[chapter~8]{levine2000linkers}. On current Linux, the kernel maps the main ELF executable and its interpreter; for an ordinary dynamic executable it is the user-space interpreter, not the kernel ELF mapper, that processes the program's dynamic symbol relocations.

The interpreter finds the dynamic table through \texttt{PT\_DYNAMIC}. The table's tags describe the resident relocation tables and their entry or byte sizes, as well as the symbol and string tables \cite[chapter~10]{levine2000linkers}. For instance, \texttt{DT\_RELASZ} measures bytes, so the count is obtained by dividing by the appropriate entry size after checking consistency. A section named \texttt{.rela.dyn} helps an inspector display those entries, but is not how the interpreter locates them. A relocation's \texttt{r\_offset} in a shared object or PIE denotes a link-time virtual address. It is not the file offset from which to read the word, nor is it the offset of the relocation record within the table.

\section{A type selects the computation}

Write $B$ for the referencing image's bias, $P$ for the runtime address of the field to change, $S$ for the resolved symbol value when needed, and $A$ for the addend. The AMD64 ABI supplement (\S4.4) gives the formulas and field widths. An ordinary x86-64 \texttt{R\_X86\_64\_RELATIVE} writes the 64-bit value $B+A$ to $P$ and needs no symbol lookup. An \texttt{R\_X86\_64\_GLOB\_DAT} writes the symbol value $S$ into a slot, often in the GOT; an \texttt{R\_X86\_64\_64} writes $S+A$. For a normal defined data symbol in another image, $S$ is the provider's bias plus the symbol's linked value. Adding the consumer's bias to $S$ would rebase a value that was already computed in runtime coordinates.

Levine's i386 examples show why the format matters as much as the formula. A \texttt{REL} record has an implicit addend in the field it describes; a \texttt{RELA} record carries an explicit addend. The AMD64 examples here use conventional \texttt{RELA}, not a packed relative relocation format. Type still matters even if two types use the same field width: a PLT jump slot has binding-time rules different from a startup data slot. Thread-local offsets and indirect function resolvers also have separate rules; neither can be modeled as an ordinary data symbol address.

