\section{Calculate two writes into one image}

This model handles only two relocation types and omits the lazy PLT pass. Interpret all address arithmetic with bounds checks and assume the destinations are designated relocation slots in private storage writable at this stage. Reject unimplemented types instead of falling back to adding the bias.

\begin{verbatim}
P = checked_add(B, r_offset)
require [P, P+8) is an allowed destination
if type == RELATIVE:
    require symbol_index == 0   # no lookup and no provider
    value = checked_add(B, r_addend)
else if type == GLOB_DAT:
    value = resolve(symbol)     # already in runtime coordinates
else:
    report unsupported type
store_le64(P, value)
\end{verbatim}

Set $B$ to \texttt{0x70000000}. A relative relocation at \texttt{0x4030} with addend \texttt{0x2050} writes \texttt{0x70002050} at \texttt{0x70004030}. Its eight little-endian bytes are \texttt{50 20 00 70 00 00 00 00}. A global-data relocation at \texttt{0x4038} resolved to a provider with bias \texttt{0x71000000} and linked value \texttt{0x3018} writes \texttt{0x71003018} at \texttt{0x70004038}. Move just the consumer up by \texttt{0x10000}, holding the provider and scope fixed. Both destinations move and the relative value moves, but the resolved global value stays the same. Also test a destination four bytes before the end of an allowed range: checking only its starting address would admit an eight-byte write extending past that range. The invariant is that type and image determine both the coordinate system of the result and the full extent of the write.

\Cref{fig:visual-293} traces the type-dependent value separately from the place where the loader writes it.

