\section{Observe the work the loader actually inherits}

Build an AMD64 fixture from \texttt{static int local = 7;} and \texttt{int *saved = \&local;}, with \texttt{cc -fPIC -shared ptr.c -o libptr.so}. Inspect \texttt{readelf -sW libptr.so} to locate the slot \texttt{saved} and then \texttt{readelf -rW libptr.so} to find the relative relocation for that slot. Other toolchain startup records are not part of the fixture. The numeric slot and addend vary; the addend should denote the linked address of \texttt{local}. The fact that the instructions are position-independent does not remove this initialized pointer.

Writes to private file mappings can dirty whole pages even when the slot itself is small. Keeping these writes out of code preserves shareable code pages. On GNU/Linux, when an image declares a \texttt{PT\_GNU\_RELRO} range, the dynamic linker makes the eligible pages read-only after relocation. Binding all eligible PLT slots early can let the link editor arrange for those slots to be in the protected range too. The final mapping permissions thus need not be identical to the writable permissions used during initialization.
